          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Ravel's Later Years</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Ravel suffered from health problems that affected his ability to compose. Despite these challenges, he continued to work, striving for perfection in his existing works and seeking to complete new compositions that would further solidify his musical legacy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Declining Health and Creativity</strong><br>\nRavel's declining health, particularly after a taxi accident in 1932, increasingly inhibited his ability to work. He composed very little in his final years and suffered from a neurological condition that made writing music difficult.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nMaurice Ravel died on December 28, 1937, in Paris. His death was mourned by the music community worldwide. He left behind a body of work that is celebrated for its originality, technical precision, and depth of expression. Ravel remains one of the most performed and recorded composers in classical music.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
